response:
Probiotics can be very effective at improving digestive conditions such as irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), ulcerative colitis, and Crohn's disease. Probiotics are beneficial bacteria that can restore intestinal flora balance and prevent pathogenic bacterial infections.
Specifically, some common probiotic strains used in clinical studies for inflammatory bowel disease include:
1) Lactobacillus acidophilus (NCFM);
2) Lactobacillus casei GG (LGG);
3) Bifidobacterium longum BB536;
4) Escherichia coli Nissle 1917. If you want to try probiotics, you can start with these probiotic products. However, there is still no definitive evidence to demonstrate the effectiveness of probiotics in treating ulcerative colitis. As far as I know, there is only one high-quality clinical study showing that it works (Pochayan, K., & Saengsuwan, J.). Therefore, please be cautious, and if your symptoms persist, please consult your doctor in time.

If you've experienced diverticulitis before, you know it can be painful but also fairly common. For mild-to-moderate attacks of diverticulitis, treatment doesn't usually require hospitalization and can be carried out at home. However, more serious attacks need emergency care because they increase your risk of a bowel perforation, leading to peritonitis or sepsis.
When dealing with diverticulitis, doctors may recommend antibiotics and clear liquids to ease pain while your intestines heal. It can take several days to recover from an attack of diverticulitis. The key is learning to recognize when diverticulitis calls for emergency care. While many people experience occasional bouts of diverticulitis, sometimes diverticulitis becomes more serious, requiring immediate attention and treatment.

The following signs that you're suffering a more serious attack of diverticulitis mean you should visit the Emergency Room (ER):

1. High fever: if you feel chills or a fever of 101°F (38°C) or higher;

2. Intense abdominal pain that starts in your lower left abdomen and moves throughout your belly;

3. Inability to pass gas, even if you are having diarrhea;

4. Severe nausea or vomiting: You can't keep food down, or you've vomited blood;

5. Abdominal distension: Your stomach swells or feels hard;

6. Bloody stools: Your bowel movements come in small amounts at a time and contain lots of blood.

These signs suggest there may be issues beyond simple inflammation and may require prompt medical intervention to treat an infection, prevent serious complications, or repair a perforated intestine. It's crucial to visit the ER and talk to a doctor if you notice worsening symptoms or new ones, as delaying treatment can lead to more severe complications.

It's also worth noting that certain foods can trigger an attack for some people with diverticulosis, including popcorn, nuts, seeds, and corn. If diverticulitis flares become frequent for you, you may want to consider adopting a modified diet or taking steps to improve your health and reduce inflammation. Consult your doctor to see how you can manage future episodes to avoid complications.
